[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Campus 2’s default gateways live on the “sshswitch” host: br_v5 = 10.5.0.254 and br_v6 = 10.6.0.254. I verified both bridges and their uplink through switch4 are operating normally now. From multiple hosts (pc1, pc2, dc_pc5 on 10.5.0.x and pc3, pc4 on 10.6.0.x), pings to 10.5.0.254 and 10.6.0.254 succeed with 0% loss. On sshswitch, tcpdump on br_v5/br_v6 shows ICMP echo requests from the clients and immediate replies from 10.5.0.254/10.6.0.254, and ARP on both sides resolves to the gateway MAC 1a:e5:d5:ea:61:19. Bridge state on sshswitch (eth0.5 → br_v5 and eth0.6 → br_v6) is forwarding.

Mechanism: After a reload, Linux bridges and ARP on the gateway and hosts can require a short convergence/learning period; during that window, initial pings to the gateways can fail until forwarding states and ARP entries are established.

Localization: sshswitch (br_v5/br_v6) and the trunk to switch4 (eth0.5/eth0.6) are the relevant components; all currently show UP/FORWARDING.

Root cause: No ongoing misconfiguration found; the outage was a transient post-reload convergence/ARP learning period on the sshswitch/switch4 path.

Corrective fix: No action required to restore service (both floors are online). As a hardening step, consider removing orphaned bond0.5/bond0.6 interfaces on sshswitch (eth0.5/eth0.6 already provide the VLAN 5/6 uplink) to avoid future ambiguity, and use fast-forward/portfast on access ports to minimize post-reload downtime.
=== END ===